I was struggling to follow this a bit, and I _think_ it's overcomplicated things a bit. I think we just need 2 steps: Copying the still-used values from the previous array over at the same index, then filling everything else in the remaining gaps. I think the following code should work (though I didn't test it), and we can get rid of the block above (`existingUsedStrings` construction) and below.

```suggestion
    // New array of undefined, ready to be filled.
    const strings = new Array(usedStrings.size).fill()
    // copy existing values to the same, capping to the max array length
    existingStrings.slice(0, usedStrings.size).forEach((s, i) => {
      if (usedStrings.has(s)) {
        strings[i] = s;
      }
    })
    // copy remaining strings into empty slots. This will also place any existing strings whose previous indices are out of the bounds of the new array.
    usedStrings.forEach((s) => {
      if (strings.indexOf(s) === -1) {
        const nextFreeSlot = strings.indexOf() // find next undefined value
        strings[nextFreeSlot] = s
      }
    })
    return strings
```

